#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175745 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91394e22-dc2a-5304-b9aa-80302de38fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Inverse Variation"}] :question/id #uuid "231b7b37-4fdc-5535-a73f-7fb743091311" :question/math-academy-id "e-1581" :question/problem "The concentration of a solution, measured in moles per liter $(\\textrm{mol}/\\textrm{L}),$ varies inversely with the volume of the solution, measured in liters $(\\textrm{L}).$ When the volume of the solution is $0.25\\,\\text{L}$, the concentration is $2 \\,\\textrm{mol}/\\textrm{L}.$ Find the concentration when the volume of the solution is $8\\,\\textrm{L}.$" :question/worked-solution "The concentration $c$ is inversely proportional to the volume $V,$ so we have\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{k}{V},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the volume $V = 0.25\\,\\textrm L,$ the concentration $c=2\\,\\textrm{mol}/\\textrm{L}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{0.25} \\\\ 2\\cdot (0.25) & =k \\\\ 0.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{0.5}{V}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the concentration when the volume  $V=8\\,\\textrm L,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{0.5}{8} = 0.0625\n$$\n\nTherefore, the concentration when $V=8\\,\\textrm{L}$ is $c=0.0625 \\,\\textrm{mol}/\\textrm{L}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31bf2725-d2ba-53a8-bbdf-0fd7cd643aca" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling with Inverse Square Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "d3520d29-1ecb-5bb0-9c1c-c530fa681853" :question/math-academy-id "e-9316" :question/problem "The irradiance $I,$ in $\\mathrm{W}/m^2,$ of a lamp placed at the center of a spherical chamber varies inversely with the **square** of the radius $r$ of the chamber, measured in meters. It is known that $I = 0.75\\,\\mathrm{W}/m^2$ when $r = 1.2\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the irradiance $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the radius $r$ of the chamber, we must have\n\n$$\nI= \\dfrac {k}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=0.75\\,\\mathrm{W}/m^2$ when $r=1.2\\,\\textrm{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75 & =\\frac{k}{{1.2}^{2}} \\\\ 0.75 & =\\frac{k}{1.44} \\\\ 0.75\\cdot 1.44 & =k \\\\ 1.08 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI= \\dfrac{1.08}{r^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "920b0639-5f8d-5b4a-b688-4326d14c2a86" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/id #uuid "c5c908e5-aa2b-5ba3-bf6c-dfa6fc49c386" :question/math-academy-id "e-9318" :question/problem "The frequency $f$, in hertz $(\\mathrm{Hz}),$ of the vibration of a violin string varies inversely with its length $L$, in meters $(\\textrm m).$ A violin string of length $0.65\\,\\mathrm{m}$ vibrates at a frequency of $f=320\\,\\textrm{Hz}.$ What is the constant of variation?" :question/worked-solution "The frequency $f$ is inversely proportional to the string length $L,$ so we have\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{k}{L},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the string length is $L = 0.65\\,\\textrm{m},$ the frequency of the string is $f = 320\\,\\textrm{Hz}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}320 & =\\frac{k}{0.65} \\\\ 320\\cdot 0.65 & =k \\\\ 208 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = fL,$ we have that the units of $k$ are $\\textrm{Hz} \\cdot \\textrm{m}.$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k = 208\\,\\textrm{Hz} \\cdot \\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "920b0639-5f8d-5b4a-b688-4326d14c2a86" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0638293e-1a8a-5087-acee-a074e3ddddac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240\\,000\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499665} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb57a0d-6854-54e3-b463-760864fdabb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499684} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb8d89e-9547-5ecc-97b1-8db11cf48265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499834} {:answer/id #uuid "6281ae21-5ac7-5f50-94b2-58ad32389f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499847} {:answer/id #uuid "7f053776-7a86-5cdb-83b4-3be937464dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480\\,000\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f053776-7a86-5cdb-83b4-3be937464dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480\\,000\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499893} :answer-field/id #uuid "8005cb6e-eaae-5313-bd28-07de59e42760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19727363-214e-5f0e-ae2c-203a88bbcec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-116238" :question/problem "A car is traveling at a constant speed on a circular road. The force $F,$ in newtons ($N$), needed to keep the car from skidding is inversely proportional with the radius $r,$ measured in meters, of the circular road. If the radius of the road is $400\\,m,$ a force of $1\\,200\\,N$ is required to prevent the car from skidding. What is the constant of variation?" :question/worked-solution "The force $F$ is inversely proportional to the radius $r$ of the road, so we have\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the radius is $r=400\\,\\text{m},$ the required force is $F=1200\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200 & =\\frac{k}{400} \\\\ 1\\,200\\cdot 400 & =k \\\\ 480\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=Fr,$ we have that the units of $k$ are $\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}.$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=480\\,000\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31bf2725-d2ba-53a8-bbdf-0fd7cd643aca" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling with Inverse Square Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0560fc46-f5b5-506e-b9cd-eb5e460856b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{0.0016}{{d}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499661} {:answer/id #uuid "0da9040c-d8ab-5e52-b3a4-a29e13964992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{0.0016}{d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499685} {:answer/id #uuid "54ab51e3-9b6a-5243-aa6c-da36f4202c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{0.016}{{d}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499818} {:answer/id #uuid "e76b2b9f-5110-51ab-b80d-dacaf30ac0c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{0.16}{{d}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500136} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ac768e-a228-569d-9660-022398decb19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1.6}{{d}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54ab51e3-9b6a-5243-aa6c-da36f4202c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{0.016}{{d}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499818} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae96d0a-2517-59d2-9013-6279cfba0795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b917a8c-b900-5585-9155-be900a8adad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-116248" :question/problem "The resistance $R,$ measured in ohms ($Ω$), of a wire is inversely proportional to the **square** of its diameter $d,$ measured in meters. A device that measures resistance registers $160Ω$ in a wire of diameter $0.01\\,m.$ What equation gives the relationship between $R$ and $d?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the resistance $R$ is inversely proportional to the square of the diameter of the wire $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nR=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $R=160\\,Ω$ when $d=0.01\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160 & =\\frac{k}{{0.01}^{2}} \\\\ 160 & =\\frac{k}{0.0001} \\\\ 160\\cdot 0.0001 & =k \\\\ 0.016 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nR=\\frac{0.016}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31bf2725-d2ba-53a8-bbdf-0fd7cd643aca" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling with Inverse Square Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0863f4-ac60-5429-b250-ea6fce3d8b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F=\\frac{218.75}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499714} {:answer/id #uuid "914a1c20-d4d4-54c3-af7b-1f2190715b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F=\\frac{253.25}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499935} {:answer/id #uuid "d2097ac8-82b6-54a5-8ac1-e988e2ee89a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F=\\frac{211.75}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500085} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c0f7eb-fc97-5361-8a70-4dde12fdd0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F=\\frac{281.25}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500089} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f6ea43-0fb5-580d-951c-f07bcfa560a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F=\\frac{205.5}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c0863f4-ac60-5429-b250-ea6fce3d8b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F=\\frac{218.75}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499714} :answer-field/id #uuid "c34c641c-ba34-5dcf-8f0d-f0fbe7d6ef67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f02660d-14fa-52e0-9b74-a7e342b95b5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-116319" :question/problem "The gravitational force $F,$ measured in kilonewtons ($kN$), that a planet exerts on an object varies inversely with the **square** of the distance $r,$ measured in megameters ($Mm$), between the object and the center of the planet. It is known that Mercury has a radius of $2.5\\,Mm$ and the gravitational force exerted on an object at Mercury's surface is $35\\,kN.$ What equation gives the relationship between $F$ and $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the gravitational force of Mercury $F$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $r,$ we must have\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $F=35\\,\\text{kN}$ when $r=2.5\\,\\text{Mm}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35 & =\\frac{k}{{2.5}^{2}} \\\\ 35 & =\\frac{k}{6.25} \\\\ 35\\cdot 6.25 & =k \\\\ 218.75 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{218.75}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91394e22-dc2a-5304-b9aa-80302de38fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Inverse Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1593b6ca-a5e6-5c6a-99e6-e8e8d2f6a953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240\\,hz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499704} {:answer/id #uuid "559e7ca7-5c48-5dbf-afb9-fd0aa26404eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "370\\,hz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499819} {:answer/id #uuid "948aa71e-20b3-5c93-8cdd-a31cc2e976b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "350\\,hz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499945} {:answer/id #uuid "b29f3ae4-96d9-5578-83d2-ac61814e0d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "325\\,hz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500012} {:answer/id #uuid "e25cb13a-fc61-5546-9491-b823b51a6910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280\\,hz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b29f3ae4-96d9-5578-83d2-ac61814e0d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "325\\,hz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500012} :answer-field/id #uuid "d600b5d7-7b72-5192-8fc3-7340cefbad77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b21f180b-3aa7-59a6-b309-e783112c781d" :question/math-academy-id "q-116412" :question/problem "The frequency $f$, in hertz ($hz$), of a guitar string's vibration is inversely proportional to its length $L,$ in meters. For a guitar string of length $0.65\\,m$ the frequency is $f=280\\,\\text{hz}.$ What will be the frequency of vibration if the string's length is reduced by $9$ centimeters?" :question/worked-solution "The frequency $f$ is inversely proportional to the string length $L,$ so we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{k}{L},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the string length is $L=0.65\\,\\text{m},$ the frequency of the string is $f=280\\,\\text{hz}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}280 & =\\frac{k}{0.65} \\\\ 280\\cdot 0.65 & =k \\\\ 182 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{182}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the frequency when the string's length is reduced by $9\\,cm,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{182}{0.65-0.09}=\\frac{182}{0.56}=325\n$$\n\nTherefore, the frequency of the string when $L=0.56\\,cm$ is $f=325\\,hz.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "920b0639-5f8d-5b4a-b688-4326d14c2a86" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9089d5-99f2-5b1c-a6ee-1ee8277b2e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499688} {:answer/id #uuid "285673bf-2869-5760-baf6-0c7f45019889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499742} {:answer/id #uuid "4aece7a0-51cc-5825-acda-7adf0e1ed06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499797} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6cf91b-6821-55aa-87ad-59a20ba393e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "700\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499801} {:answer/id #uuid "8627984e-d902-5093-8c7c-0cb13ae5af72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9089d5-99f2-5b1c-a6ee-1ee8277b2e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499688} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f0ff7f-2fda-5d56-9867-4253599e5ea1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3fd4767-8599-5ef5-a83e-e33c37e90d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-120397" :question/problem "The number of sales of a new brand of cellphone varies inversely with its price, in dollars. A store sells $100$ units of this new brand of cellphone when its price is $$700.$ What is the constant of variation?" :question/worked-solution "The number of sales $n$ is inversely proportional to the price $P,$ so we have\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{k}{P},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when its price is $$700,$ the store sells $100$ units. We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =\\frac{k}{700} \\\\ 100\\cdot 700 & =k \\\\ 70\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=70\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91394e22-dc2a-5304-b9aa-80302de38fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Inverse Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7fa420-1d79-570c-bbb3-bc424501fc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$100$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499963} {:answer/id #uuid "ad359108-8f03-517f-8245-985fb1a36f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$33$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500000} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a89ac5-3373-55ed-b103-cb94f2882651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$333$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500032} {:answer/id #uuid "c50b9545-66ce-5a7b-a181-7a4ac4342137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500053} {:answer/id #uuid "cb42dbb4-9153-570f-bfcb-8a7721299b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$250$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a89ac5-3373-55ed-b103-cb94f2882651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$333$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500032} :answer-field/id #uuid "84287a8c-1b88-5932-82df-43aa4abcbc9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d23aaba0-389e-51c2-b2cb-f09afbfdf0ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-120415" :question/problem "Mark's expenditure of money $E$, in dollars, is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ also in dollars. If Mark spends $$500$ when he saves $$200,$ what will be Mark's expenditure (to the nearest dollar) when he saves $$300?$" :question/worked-solution "Mark's expenditure of money $E$ is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ so we have\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{k}{S},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that Mark spends $$500$ when he saves $$200.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500 & =\\frac{k}{200} \\\\ 500\\cdot 200 & =k \\\\ 100\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{S}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find Mark's expenditure when he saves $$300,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{300}≈333\n$$\n\nTherefore,  Mark's expenditure when  $S=$300$ is $E=$333.$"}]}
